      "solution": "$\\int (2\\sin(x-3) + e^{3x})\\,dx = 2(-\\cos(x-3)) + \\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + c = -2\\cos(x-3) + \\frac{1}{3}e^{3x} + c$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Sum rule for integration: integrate each term independently using $\\int \\sin(ax+b) dx = -\\frac{1}{a}\\cos(ax+b)$ and $\\int e^{kx} dx = \\frac{1}{k}e^{kx}$.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Integrate $2\\sin(x-3)$ with respect to $x$ to get $-2\\cos(x-3)$.",
          "Integrate $e^{3x}$ with respect to $x$ to get $\\frac{1}{3}e^{3x}$.",
          "Combine terms and add $c$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Incorrect sign when integrating sine, mistaking it for $+\\cos$."
      }
